## Summary

Fixed the reverse-shell false positive in src/safety.rs (#578).

The bug: check_reverse_shell matched ["nc ", "ncat ", "netcat "] as bare substrings. `rsync  contains the substring nc  (from "rsy**nc **"), and rsync -c foo bar` supplies the -c flag the check also looks for — so a routine rsync got flagged as a reverse shell. A real product footgun for any user.

The fix: Reworked the netcat check to match nc/ncat/netcat only as standalone command tokens, reusing the existing `is_at_word_boundary helper (which treats whitespace, ;, |, &, (, and /` as boundaries — so full-path invocations like /usr/bin/nc -e ... still catch). Scans all occurrences with a char-boundary-safe `find` loop; no byte-slicing at arbitrary offsets.

**Tests (Day 122 discipline — verify the silent side):**
• Positive still flags: nc -e /bin/sh, `ncat -c 'bash -i', cd /tmp; nc -e ..., foo | netcat -e ...`
• Near-misses that must NOT flag: rsync -c foo bar (the reported bug), rsync -e ssh src dst, `./configure -c && make, franchise -e list`

All checks green: cargo build, cargo test (88 passed), `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`.

